==============================================================================================================================
==========================================================Autobackup==========================================================
==============================================================================================================================
Autobackup is an RMAN feature.
It is used to automatically back up both the Control File and the SPFILE.

Check whether it is enabled:

rman target /
RMAN> SHOW CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP;
or
RMAN> SHOW ALL;

By default, CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP is ON in Oracle 19c (unless it has been changed).

------------------------------------------------------------

When is Autobackup created?

Autobackup is created automatically after any successful RMAN backup, such as:

- Full Backup
- Incremental Level 0 Backup
- Incremental Level 1 Backup
- Archivelog Backup

Note:
Incremental Level 0 is NOT the same as Full Backup.
In our database, the backup level depends on the RMAN backup schedule.
It can be either Incremental Level 0 or Incremental Level 1 depending on the backup strategy.

--How to determine whether a backup piece belongs to an Autobackup or a regular backup
SELECT backup_type,
       incremental_level,
       controlfile_included,
       completion_time
FROM v$backup_set
WHERE backup_type='D'
ORDER BY completion_time DESC;

SELECT backup_type,
       incremental_level,
       controlfile_included,
       completion_time
FROM v$backup_set
ORDER BY completion_time DESC;
D = Datafile Backup
L = Archived Log Backup
I = Incremental Backup

if:
BACKUP_TYPE = I
and INCREMENTAL_LEVEL = 0
   ⇒ Incremental Level 0.
if:
BACKUP_TYPE = I
and INCREMENTAL_LEVEL = 1
  ⇒ Incremental Level 1.

SELECT backup_type,
       incremental_level,
       COUNT(*)
FROM v$backup_set
GROUP BY backup_type, incremental_level
ORDER BY backup_type;

------------------------------------------------------------

Where is the Autobackup stored?
You can configure the destination.

It can be stored in:
- ASM Disk Group (for example: +FRA)
- Fast Recovery Area (filesystem)
- Any custom path using FORMAT

Example:
CONFIGURE CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP FORMAT FOR DEVICE TYPE DISK
TO '/backup/%F';

------------------------------------------------------------

Autobackup file names
If Oracle Managed Files (OMF) is used, Oracle generates the file name automatically.

Example:

o1_mf_s_1236543825_o3vjyp2c_.bkp

Explanation:
o1                  -> Oracle internal prefix
mf                  -> Managed File (OMF)
s_1236543825        -> Backup Set Stamp
o3vjyp2c            -> Unique identifier generated by Oracle
.bkp                -> Backup Piece

Or Oracle may generate names like:
+FRA/DB1/AUTOBACKUP/2026_07_29/s_1236543825.1113.1239870681

------------------------------------------------------------

All Autobackups are registered in the RMAN Repository.
The RMAN Repository can be stored in the Control File or in a Recovery Catalog.
You can query them using RMAN commands or Dynamic Performance Views.

RMAN commands:
LIST BACKUP OF DATABASE;
LIST BACKUP OF CONTROLFILE;
LIST BACKUP OF SPFILE;

------------------------------------------------------------

--SQL queries
--Check the backup type and incremental level
SELECT bp.handle,
       bs.controlfile_included,
       bs.backup_type,
       bs.incremental_level
FROM v$backup_set bs
JOIN v$backup_piece bp
ON bs.set_stamp = bp.set_stamp
AND bs.set_count = bp.set_count
WHERE bp.handle LIKE '%o1_mf_s_1236543825%';

Meaning:

CONTROLFILE_INCLUDED
YES -> The Backup Set contains the Control File.
NO -> The Backup Set does not contain the Control File.

BACKUP_TYPE
D -> Datafile Backup
L -> Archived Log Backup

INCREMENTAL_LEVEL
NULL -> Not an Incremental Backup (could be Full Backup, Control File Backup, SPFILE Backup, or Archivelog Backup)
0 -> Incremental Level 0
1 -> Incremental Level 1

------------------------------------------------------------

--To verify that the Backup Set also contains the SPFILE:

SELECT *
FROM v$backup_spfile
WHERE set_stamp = 1236913925;


